{
	"_help": "dirt_shift configuration. Shared values (the timer file path and the battery's cell count) are read from zeroinput.conf — not duplicated here. This file holds only the path to zeroinput.conf and the dirt_shift-specific parameters. Grid data for the zones comes from the Energy-Charts 'Stromampel' (Fraunhofer ISE, CC BY 4.0), or from SMARD day-ahead data if that is unavailable — one source per run, never mixed. If both fail, cached data substitutes for one more day, provided it holds real values for today; after that zones are modelled from the weather forecast (emergency mode, clearly flagged). Only if the weather is unavailable too does dirt_shift abort and leave an all-allowed timer.",
	"zeroinput_conf": "../zeroinput.conf",
	"zeroinput_conf_help": "path to zeroinput.conf (dirt_shift usually lives in a subfolder of zeroinput). discharge_t_file (the timer path) is read from there.",
	"vz_host_port": "192.168.1.10:8080",
	"vz_host_port_help": "volkszaehler.org host:port for the data.json API (dirt_shift uses the HTTP API, separate from zeroinput's vzlogger FIFO).",
	"vz_chans": {
		"Inverter": "00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001",
		"Import": "00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000002",
		"Auto": "00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000003",
		"PV": "00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000006",
		"Vbat": "00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000007"
	},
	"vz_chans_help": "volkszaehler.org channel UUIDs. basic_load consumption = Import + |Inverter| - Auto (adapt the formula in get_average and this channel set to your own installation). PV feeds the PV reference curve, the battery content and the measured PV of the last quarter hour (energy budget, wallbox voltage path); Vbat feeds the empty-battery anchor of the content and the wallbox voltage path.",
	"vz_dirtiness_uuid": "",
	"vz_dirtiness_uuid_help": "optional: real volkszaehler.org channel UUID (created in volkszaehler.org beforehand) for the current grid dirtiness value. dirt_shift POSTs the value directly to volkszaehler.org's middleware API once per run — http://{vz_host_port}/data/{this UUID}.json — no vzlogger meter or local file involved. Sign convention matches the installation's existing power channels (Import positive = drawing from the grid, Inverter negative = feeding in): positive = renewables cover less than the load, negative = renewable surplus (renewables exceed the load). Empty disables the write.",
	"average_days": 7,
	"average_days_help": "days of history for the hourly basic_load/PV average. 7 = one full week structure. Cached hourly in dirt_avg_cache.json.",
	"day_weights_pct": [
		200,
		100,
		100,
		100,
		100,
		150,
		200
	],
	"day_weights_pct_help": "per-day weighting (percent) for the average, chronological: index 0 = oldest day (today minus average_days, i.e. same weekday last week), index -1 = yesterday. Weighting yesterday and last week's same weekday higher captures recent trend and weekday structure. Length must equal average_days; on mismatch all days are weighted equally. All 100 = neutral. In the web form, edit as a comma-separated list.",
	"reserve_pct": 90,
	"reserve_pct_help": "percent of the demand to hold back as the red reserve, taken as max(red_window_demand, upcoming_red_demand): the first sums every red hour between now and the next PV surplus phase, the second the next contiguous red block even across a surplus, minus the surplus expected before that block begins (surplus_before_next_red) — the second is the floor that keeps the reserve from reading 0 while now sits inside a surplus stretch, while the subtraction keeps it from holding the battery idle through a morning whose own yield already covers the gap. ~90 so the reserve is nearly used up by the time PV production resumes. Values above 100 are allowed and make the reserve itself more cautious. Also scales the quarter-hour energy budget of non-priority red hours (see limit_discharge_rate_help), where it is capped at 100% — above that a cap would exceed the hour's own net demand and stop restricting anything — and the wallbox-specific reserve check, which uses the same max() (see wallbox_typical_power_help).",
	"limit_discharge_rate": 3000,
	"limit_discharge_rate_help": "Watt cap on battery discharge power during a non-priority red hour (content below reserve, this hour not the dirtiest in the window) — installation-specific, adapt to the ordinary peak load of your own house. Applies together with a fixed quarter-hour energy budget (1/4 * (min(1, reserve_pct) * basic_load - measured PV), floored at 0, not configurable — scaled by reserve_pct like the reserve calculation itself, but netted against the PV actually measured over the last 15 minutes rather than the forecast's hourly mean, which is too coarse on the ramp hours that are routinely the marginal red ones): the rate cap alone would still let a small but sustained excess load through over the full hour, the energy budget alone would still let a brief high-power spike through. Set very high to make the rate cap effectively unrestricted, leaving only the energy budget in effect.",
	"latitude": 51.0,
	"longitude": 10.0,
	"PV_to_bat_efficiency": 98,
	"bat_to_AC_efficiency": 90,
	"max_days_empty_battery": 7,
	"max_days_empty_battery_help": "how many days back to search for a 'battery empty' state (voltage at or below 3.0625 V per cell, 49 V at 16S, for at least 2 minutes) as the integration anchor.",
	"battery_capacity_wh": 5000,
	"battery_capacity_wh_help": "usable battery capacity in Wh. Used to tell whether the battery would fill up on its own before the next dirty phase: a green-hour stop is turned into a limit when the held-back charge plus the coming surplus would exceed it (otherwise PV would be curtailed), and precharge only diverts into room that remains after the natural surplus. Also drives the state-of-charge display and the negative-content warning. Not read from zeroinput.conf, which does not need it. A rough value is fine; it gates decisions, it is not a hard charge limit. 0 disables the capacity-based checks.",
	"disable_zeroinput_timer": false,
	"disable_zeroinput_timer_help": "set true to compute and print without writing the timer file — no timer write at all, the all-allowed fallback after an abort included. Only the battery side goes unsteered: an enabled wallbox is still switched. The battery-side cap while the wallbox runs on the dirt path alone (free->limit) then has no effect either; the energy path still checks the reserve.",
	"logfile": "",
	"logfile_help": "optional path to a logfile that mirrors every console print — empty disables it. Setting this alone already forces verbose output (as if -v were passed), console output included, since a configured logfile is itself the request for the verbose record; -debug on the command line still adds the hourly table on top. Resolved relative to dirt_shift.py's directory unless absolute. Opened in append mode, one run after another with a timestamp line between them; dirt_shift never rotates or truncates it itself, so an external tool (e.g. logrotate) should if it is left on permanently. A logfile that can't be opened or written only prints a warning — it never blocks writing timer.txt.",
	"latitude_help": "decimal degrees (north) of the installation, for the clear-sky irradiance model (clear_sky_ghi) and the Open-Meteo radiation forecast. Default ~centre of Germany.",
	"longitude_help": "decimal degrees (east) of the installation, for the same clear-sky model and radiation forecast. Default ~centre of Germany.",
	"PV_to_bat_efficiency_help": "percent efficiency PV -> battery, used to reconstruct the real battery energy content (see get_vz_bat_cap). Set too low (more pessimistic than the real installation), the reconstructed content drifts negative over time; a persistent 'battery content came out ... below empty' warning suggesting an efficiency cause (not a hardware one) is the sign to check this and bat_to_AC_efficiency against a genuine empty-to-empty cycle.",
	"bat_to_AC_efficiency_help": "percent efficiency battery -> AC/inverter, used the same way as PV_to_bat_efficiency — see its help for how a too-low value shows up (the negative-content warning) and how to recalibrate it.",
	"precharge_enabled": false,
	"precharge_enabled_help": "optional and off by default: while true, in the cleanest green hours of the window dirt_shift may cap the timer's ac_% below 100, so PV that would otherwise feed the house goes into the battery and the house draws that share from the grid — the one deliberate exception to the pvpt guarantee. The trade: a clean grid import now for more charge in the dirty red block later, paid for with the round-trip loss. It only helps while the battery would NOT be full by the red block anyway (if the natural surplus fills it regardless, diverting just fills it earlier and more PV is curtailed later), so it engages only when (1) the hour ranks within precharge_cleanest_pct % of the window, (2) the red block is dirtier by at least the round-trip loss times round_trip_margin, and (3) room remains in the battery after the natural surplus and the red block could use more. It diverts at most that remaining room, spread across the qualifying hours, never below an ac_% of 20. Only real grid data counts: the precharge window ends at the last published hour (substitute values for a tomorrow not yet published are ignored), and in emergency mode precharge is off. See precharge_ac_pct in the spec.",
	"precharge_cleanest_pct": 25,
	"precharge_cleanest_pct_help": "precharge only throttles green hours ranking among the cleanest this-many percent of the rolling 24-hour window (default 25). A rank like wallbox_cleanest_pct, not a fraction of a signed dirt%. Raise it to precharge in more hours, lower it to restrict to the cleanest few.",
	"round_trip_margin": 1.5,
	"round_trip_margin_help": "cost versus cleanliness setting for precharge. Precharge diverts PV through the battery, which costs its round-trip loss (from PV_to_bat_efficiency and bat_to_AC_efficiency) in extra grid import; the red block must be dirtier than the current hour by at least that loss times this factor. 1.0 = divert as soon as it is a net CO2 gain (cleanest, most grid import and battery cycling); larger values demand a clearer gain first (cheaper, fewer cycles); a very large value effectively turns precharge off. Values below 1.0 count as 1.0. Has no effect on the discharge decisions, which shift already stored energy and so involve no extra round trip.",
	"wallbox_enabled": false,
	"wallbox_enabled_help": "optional and off by default: while true, dirt_shift switches an EV wallbox's Tasmota relay on during sufficiently clean hours and off again outside them, but only ever switches off a relay it switched on itself (see the owner marker, dirt_wallbox_marker.json) — a manual activation is never undone by dirt_shift, whatever the grid does; once dirt_shift itself sets the relay on during a later clean hour, it is treated as its own from then on. Three independent conditions switch it on (any one suffices, all three must fail to switch it off): the two dirtiness thresholds below (the discharge mode plays no part in it; while the wallbox runs without the battery having surplus of its own, main() instead downgrades a 'free' discharge mode to 'limit', capping battery discharge to the house's share so the car's draw lands on the grid), enough battery content against a wallbox-specific reserve (see wallbox_typical_power_help), or a full battery with actual PV surplus — voltage not having dropped below WALLBOX_V_ON_PER_CELL over the last quarter hour while measured PV exceeds the house load, so otherwise curtailed PV goes into the car (named constants in the code, scaled by cell_count, with a slightly lower holding threshold as a narrow hysteresis). No presence/charging detection is used: the relay is simply held closed whenever the hour qualifies, on-command verified via Tasmota's own relay status (up to 3 attempts, 30 s apart) before the marker is set.",
	"wallbox_ip": "",
	"wallbox_ip_help": "IP address of the Tasmota device controlling the wallbox relay.",
	"wallbox_output": "1",
	"wallbox_output_help": "Tasmota relay/output number (Power1, Power2, ...) — '1' for a single-relay device's first (usually only) output.",
	"wallbox_cleanest_pct": 30,
	"wallbox_cleanest_pct_help": "the wallbox may only be on while the current hour ranks among the cleanest this-many percent of the rolling 24-hour window (default 30 = only the cleanest 30% of hours). A rank, not a share of the median: dirt% is signed and goes negative whenever renewables exceed load, so a percentage of it would mean something different on every day — from letting nothing through on a uniformly dirty day to letting most of the window through on a windy one. As a rank the setting always admits exactly that share of hours. Works together with wallbox_absolute_max, which stays an absolute ceiling.",
	"wallbox_absolute_max": 50,
	"wallbox_absolute_max_help": "the wallbox may only be on while the current hour's dirt% is at or below this absolute value (default 50) — prevents wallbox_cleanest_pct alone from still allowing a meaningfully dirty hour on a uniformly dirty day (high median). Both this and wallbox_cleanest_pct must hold (AND, not OR).",
	"wallbox_typical_power": 2000,
	"wallbox_typical_power_help": "typical wallbox charging power in Watt, used only to project ahead for the energy safety net (see wallbox_decide): one margin is wallbox_typical_power * SLOT_HOURS, a single slot's worth of draw, and (content - headroom) is compared against a reserve computed exactly as the main one is, max(red_window_demand, upcoming_red_demand) — the wallbox is never held to a lower bar than the battery it draws from — both to decide whether a comfortably full battery may start the wallbox even outside the dirt% thresholds, and to decide when a currently-running (dirt_shift-owned) wallbox must be switched off before it would eat into the reserve. Switching on demands WALLBOX_ENERGY_ON_FACTOR margins of headroom, holding only one (hysteresis: a switch-on decided on less than one run of draw would be undone by its own consequence at the next run). Does not need to match the real charging current exactly — it is a safety margin, not a metered value."
}
